// #95 — THE ROOT OF THE #68 CLASS. `types_compatible`'s "Struct → &dyn Trait
// coercion (impl check deferred to codegen)" arm is a BLANKET ACCEPT, and the
// Tuple / Array / generic-instance arms walk an aggregate ELEMENTWISE straight
// into it. So a value ALREADY TYPED as the thin aggregate is accepted where the
// fat one is wanted, NO coercion is ever attempted (there is no literal at the
// use site to stamp), and the consumer reads a fat pair out of a thin one.
//
// The answer is REFUSAL, not a wider coercion: Rust does not coerce inside an
// aggregate value either. What Rust DOES do — propagate an expectation into an
// aggregate LITERAL at a coercion site — is `retype_aggregate_lit_to`, and it
// runs BEFORE this refusal, from `coerce_arg_to_param`. That ORDER is the whole
// distinction: a literal has been stamped by the time the check runs, so only a
// value with no literal to carry the expectation is refused. The admit half is
// pinned in tests/logos/pass/aggregate_unsize_literal_and_cast_admit.logos.
// GENERIC STRUCT FIELD. The mismatch surfaces on the INSTANCE, not the field:
// MEASURED, the field's own `expect_type` sees `(&Sq,i64) <- (&Sq,i64)` (the
// field type is the TypeVar's inferred binding), and only `G<(&dyn Shape,i64)>
// <- G<(&Sq,i64)>` carries the question — hence the type-argument arm of the
// walk, and the `<0>` in the slot path. Probe h_genfield.logos (rc=139).
package test;
trait Shape { fn area(&self) -> i64; }
trait Other { fn tag(&self) -> i64; }
struct Sq { s: i64 }
impl Shape for Sq { fn area(&self) -> i64 { return self.s * self.s; } }
struct G<T> { v: T }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: Sq = Sq { s: 4i64 };
    let t = (&a, 7i64);
    let g: G<(&dyn Shape, i64)> = G { v: t };
    return g.v.0.area() as i32;
}
